      "description": "The player's reasoning says 'deploy the second Mox Opal first for metalcraft progress, then cast the creature,' but they chose Pinnacle Emissary before casting the second Mox Opal or Urza's Bauble. Casting Urza's Bauble first (a free artifact) would give them 3 artifacts (Island artifact land? No - Island is a basic land, not an artifact. They have Lotus Petal and Mox Opal on board). Actually, looking at the board: Island, Lotus Petal, Mox Opal = 2 artifacts. Casting Urza's Bauble first would make 3 artifacts, enabling Metalcraft on Mox Opal for mana. Casting Mox Opal second would be legendary and sacrifice one due to legend rule. But more importantly, casting Pinnacle Emissary first means its trigger 'Whenever you cast an artifact spell, create a 1/1 Drone token' would miss the Urza's Bauble and second Mox Opal casts. If they cast Pinnacle Emissary first, then cast Urza's Bauble and/or Mox Opal after, they'd get Drone tokens from each artifact cast. Wait - but they're casting Emissary via Warp for {U/R}, sacrificing Lotus Petal for the mana. After Emissary resolves, they could cast Urza's Bauble (free) and get a 1/1 Drone token. So actually casting Emissary first IS correct for maximizing tokens. But casting the second Mox Opal would cause legend rule sacrifice. The sequencing of casting Emissary first to then trigger off subsequent artifact casts is actually correct. However, they sacrificed Lotus Petal for mana - they could have tapped Mox Opal if they had metalcraft. With only 2 artifacts (Lotus Petal + Mox Opal), no metalcraft. So Lotus Petal sacrifice was necessary. After Emissary resolves, they can cast Urza's Bauble to get a token. This seems reasonable actually.",
